{
 "ask": "Kenne (the owner) on the Kith Quest score, verbatim: \"we need to run these through composer and producer council, i want them to be more lush more orchestrated and beautiful, full realised score, high and classical and cinematic pieces, lush string and orchestral movements\". Earlier goals still stand: \"great but catchy … create new classic video game themes\", lullabies \"for adults\", enchanting, whimsical fantasy.\nKith Quest is an original 2D fantasy platformer: a small lantern-bearer crosses Glowmoss Hollow (mossy glowing forest), Cascade Heights (waterfalls, sky) and Lanternkeep (an old moonlit keep), ending in the Hollow Warden boss.\nCurrent state: 12 cues written as single-line parts with block-chord pads (each chord voiced as one sustained stack), a harp/ARP figure, and a few solo winds. Measurements of the current masters (band energy shares, stereo, dynamics) are attached; they show what a listener hears: mostly flat 1–5 dB dynamic range, lots of 250–800 Hz, little string movement.",
 "constraints": [
  "Original music only: no quoting or imitating any existing game or film theme. Name composers only as schools of craft.",
  "Keep the LANTERN MOTIF (sol·do–re–mi, C5 . F5 G5 A5 in F) as the game-wide leitmotif and keep its transformations (minor for the Warden/danger, phrygian for fear).",
  "The three calm lullabies (calm-glowmoss, calm-cascade, calm-keep) are melodies Kenne already loves: keep their tunes note for note; everything around them (harmony colour, counterpoint, orchestration, form) may grow.",
  "Loops must stay seamless game loops (same tempo throughout; body length any whole number of bars; a loop can be longer to hold real movements: up to ~3 minutes).",
  "clear and gameover are one-shot jingles (under ~12 s).",
  "Moods are switched live by the game (calm ↔ energy per stage, danger, fear at 1 HP, battle for the boss): matching cues share key and tempo relationships so crossfades land.",
  "No bell-type voices (see palette).",
  "Every recommendation must be buildable with the palette above: name presets, registers (note names), bar ranges, dynamics.",
  "Use the full Chora suite: every cue should feature TONOS world strings and SOMA percussion where they serve the music (e.g. kora/koto/lute ostinatos and colours beside the orchestra), not only LYRA."
 ],
 "palette": "Kenne: the cues are written and composed IN CHORA using the FULL suite of Chora instruments, including SOMA and the new plucked string instrument TONOS. Every part is a Chora instrument track; use the whole suite where it serves the music.\nLYRA (physical-model orchestra): lyra-silk-strings (long-bow violin section), lyra-string-quartet, lyra-concert-violin, lyra-viola, lyra-cello, lyra-double-bass-arco, lyra-pizzicato, lyra-sul-tasto (airy bowed), lyra-harp, lyra-concert-flute, lyra-alto-flute, lyra-piccolo, lyra-oboe, lyra-english-horn, lyra-clarinet, lyra-bass-clarinet, lyra-bassoon, lyra-french-horn, lyra-trumpet-open, lyra-trumpet-cup, lyra-flugelhorn, lyra-trombone, lyra-bass-trombone, lyra-tuba, lyra-felt (felt piano), lyra-concert-grand, lyra-tin-whistle, lyra-nylon, lyra-mandolin.\nTONOS (NEW: 52 physically modelled world string instruments, real string tension/body physics, sympathetic strings): plucked/hammered/bowed — tonos-kantele, tonos-kantele-bright, tonos-koto, tonos-guzheng, tonos-guzheng-glide, tonos-gayageum, tonos-gayageum-nonghyeon, tonos-pipa, tonos-pipa-lunzhi (tremolo), tonos-kora, tonos-kora-sesse, tonos-kamale-ngoni, tonos-valiha, tonos-lute, tonos-oud, tonos-oud-taqsim, tonos-qanun, tonos-santur, tonos-yangqin, tonos-cimbalom, tonos-cimbalom-dampers, tonos-bouzouki-tremolo, tonos-balalaika-tremolo, tonos-portuguese-guitar, tonos-baglama, tonos-sitar, tonos-tanpura-drone, tonos-hardanger (fiddle with sympathetics), tonos-nyckelharpa, tonos-hurdy-gurdy, tonos-hurdy-gurdy-coups, tonos-erhu-singing, tonos-morin-khuur, tonos-kamancheh, tonos-sarangi, tonos-esraj. Gut/silk/nylon-strung voices (koto, kora, lute, gayageum, oud, pipa, valiha) are the safest for warmth; steel hammered dulcimers (cimbalom, santur, yangqin) and bright kantele must stay sparing and soft so nothing reads as a metallic bell.\nVELA suite (sustained voices): VELA choir, vox-humana, tremolo-strings, hollow, aurora/aerial/cathedral (sustained pads), vespers/rise (pads); CANTUS voices: cantus-vault (cathedral choir), cantus-plainsong, cantus-byzantine; ISON drones: ison-bourdon, ison-shruti, ison-cavern; PNEUMA breath winds: pneuma-ney, pneuma-shakuhachi, pneuma-hall.\nSOMA (physically modelled percussion, 36 drums): timpani-29-dresden / timpani-32-profondo / timpani-23-piccolo / timpani-pedal-gliss / timpani-staccato-wood (tuned per bar), concert-gran-cassa, orchestral-field-snare, concert-tom-mahogany, gong-drum-boobam, nagado-daiko-odaiko, shime-daiko-tense, bodhran-kerry-tipper, bendir-atlas-gut, tar-nubian-deep, daf-persian-rings, ocean-frame-brush, shamanic-cedar-beater, kanjira-lizard-frame, darbuka, tabla, udu-igbo-clay, djembe, talking drum.\nGenerative: Chora ARP device (order/rate/octaves/euclidean accents, scale-locked), Humanize, modulators (LFO to pan/volume/mod-wheel swell), performDoc (cinematic rubato feel), volume automation lanes per track (hairpins, swells, terraced dynamics).\nMixing: per-track gain/pan/volume automation, 2 send buses: FX1 Spaces hall (Grand Concert Hall / Stone Chapel / Opera House), FX2 dotted-8th delay. No per-track EQ/filters in this pipeline: fix mud by voicing, register and orchestration.\nTracks are unlimited: divisi string sections are stacked LYRA tracks (Violins I / Violins II / Violas / Celli / Basses), each its own voice-led line (the engine voice-leads chords into these four upper lines with suspensions automatically), pan and hall send.\nFORBIDDEN by the owner: any bell-type voice (celesta, glockenspiel, tubular bells, struck glass, VELA struck family himalayan-bronze/vitreous/ferrous/deep-wash, glass choir) — Kenne heard a metallic sheen from them.",
